  "summary": "Distinctive architecture developed by wealthy merchants in the Huizhou region of Anhui Province.",
  "description": "Characterized by white walls, black tiles, intricate wood carvings, and imposing horseshoe-shaped entrance gates. The style reflects the prosperity of Huizhou merchants and their Confucian values, blending practicality with artistic refinement.",
  "culturalContext": "Huizhou merchant houses (Ming-Qing Anhui province) — white-washed horse-head walls over dark timber frames — were the homes of the salt-and-pawn bankers whose Hui merchants guild financed them: multi-story courtyard houses (tian jing skywells) that funnelled light and rain (\"four waters return to the hall\" = wealth retained), carved brick gate facades (menlou), and family hierarchy encoded in hall placement. Xidi and Hongcun villages are UNESCO-listed; the white-wall/black-tile image became \"Anhui style\" and China's ink-painting architecture.",
